1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
The Garage.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
A repository for tools and gadgets designed to efficiently conquer every home and garden task.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Their appearance is common.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Their stories are unique.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, Garage Gadgets on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:48,271 --> 00:00:51,220
More than a place to park your car,

7
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:56,380
the garage has evolved into the at-home sanctuary for the modern American male.

8
00:00:57,060 --> 00:01:01,140
It is also the place where the tools of 21st century home maintenance are kept,

9
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:04,080
our familiar yet beloved garage gadgets.

10
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:07,740
The garage is becoming our favorite place.

11
00:01:07,741 --> 00:01:11,720
It's really the last place that we men have any control over.

12
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:15,100
It's a place where we keep our toys, our equipment,

13
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:17,900
and equipment is the key word because we love equipment.

14
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:23,160
While most men consider themselves to be handy around the house,

15
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:29,500
Fathers see it as their role to provide for and take care of the family's home and possessions.

16
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:33,880
I think we go all the way back to the caveman and, you know, we beat our chest.

17
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:36,500
We are the defenders. We are the protectors.

18
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,920
And that also means that in addition to protecting,

19
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:44,140
we have to fix the stuff that we broke in the process of defending and protecting.

20
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:49,140
The gadgets found in most garages are so common you might not notice them.

21
00:01:49,141 --> 00:01:55,960
but their invention and evolution are tied to the individual sagas experienced by homeowners throughout history.

22
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:02,520
The barns and carriage houses of the 1890s became America's first garages,

23
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:05,320
as the automobile chased the animals out,

24
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:07,940
and as portrayed in these early silent films,

25
00:02:07,941 --> 00:02:15,320
the garage assumed the role as the household headquarters for the challenges and opportunities posed by the four seasons of the year.

26
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:24,800
In summertime, the lawnmower is king.

27
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:34,800
Just as we keep our lawnmowers in garages, mankind's first lawnmowers were appropriately kept in barns.

28
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:48,140
Grass constitutes one quarter of the Earth's vegetation and is the staple food source for many of the animals domesticated by man.

29
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:52,540
For about 25,000 years, men didn't have lawns.

30
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:58,720
They just had great expanses of field, and, you know, they were hunters or gatherers, and there was no reason.

31
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:04,620
But along about the 1600s, very rich people got the idea that they would have a lawn.

32
00:03:04,621 --> 00:03:07,580
They would actually just hammer the grass down.

33
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:09,480
They wouldn't cut it.

34
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:14,980
And then when they did start cutting it, of course, the rich people didn't cut it.

35
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:18,460
They had their servants, and they had these swinging sickles.

36
00:03:18,461 --> 00:03:24,200
and it would take a man with a swinging sickle three hours to do a third of an acre.

37
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:27,480
And some of these lawns at that time were 10, 12 acres,

38
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:29,960
so you'd have to have a whole serving staff.

39
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:37,800
These greenskeepers also used a curved metal cutting device called a scythe.

40
00:03:40,980 --> 00:03:42,900
After wetting the lawn to weigh it down,

41
00:03:42,901 --> 00:03:47,620
they would swing the scythe back and forth in an attempt to cut the grass as evenly as possible.

42
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:59,400
But even before the animals were sharing space with the automobile, the lawn mower was being invented.

43
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:09,380
In 1830, Englishman Edwin Budding patented the first machine for mowing lawns, etc.

44
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:17,640
Budding worked at a textile mill, and his reel or cylinder lawn mower was modeled from a rotary shearing machine,

45
00:04:17,641 --> 00:04:20,580
used at the mill to cut the fuzz off cotton cloth.

46
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:23,880
It was a real kind of machine.

47
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:26,800
He adapted it and made it a big 19-inch blade

48
00:04:26,801 --> 00:04:29,540
and took it out and you pushed it.

49
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:32,280
It was just all hand-powered.

50
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:34,640
And this thing would turn and would actually cut the lawn.

51
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:36,260
That was the very first lawnmowers.

52
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:41,800
Adaptations to Budding's original design came in many forms.

53
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:46,140
In 1842, a Scotsman named Alexander Shanks

54
00:04:46,141 --> 00:04:49,560
invented the first effective non-human powered lawnmower.

55
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:53,240
A horse-drawn machine called Shank's Pony.

56
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:57,260
The pony worked, but the horse tended to leave a mess.

57
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:01,520
In Britain and the U.S.,

58
00:05:01,521 --> 00:05:04,140
the simple design of Budding's hand-pushed reel mower

59
00:05:04,141 --> 00:05:06,180
made it a target for copycats.

60
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:10,560
By the 1850s, lawnmowers were being mass-produced

61
00:05:10,561 --> 00:05:13,540
as their popularity increased and price dropped.

62
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:21,240
Inventors and manufacturers introduced steam-powered mowers in the late 1800s.

63
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:25,980
Some weighed over two tons, a bit large for the average homeowner.

64
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:35,520
Then in 1919 came the breakthrough moment in lawnmower history.

65
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:42,480
An American Army colonel, Edwin George, took the gasoline motor off his wife's washing machine

66
00:05:42,481 --> 00:05:45,420
and installed it onto his roller lawnmower,

67
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:49,340
creating the first gasoline-powered lawnmower in America.

68
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:52,360
Although his clothes may have been less than clean,

69
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:54,240
Colonel George had made history.

70
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:56,760
The age of the power mower had begun.

71
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:01,640
For several years, do-it-yourselfers built their own power mowers.

72
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:07,400
Until 1934, when the Evinrude Company introduced the Lawn Boy.

73
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:11,020
With the small Briggs & Stratton gasoline engine,

74
00:06:11,021 --> 00:06:14,780
It was the first power reel mower introduced to the American consumer.

75
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:26,160
After World War II, the expanse of lawns of suburbia spurred the development of more powerful, streamlined lawnmowers.

76
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,980
When all the servicemen came home and the home building boom hit,

77
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:35,120
and that's when the power lawnmower really came into its own.

78
00:06:35,121 --> 00:06:38,000
Up until that time, lawns were smaller.

79
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:42,780
They used real lawnmowers, the term real, that type,

80
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:47,080
where the rotary power mower, which became more efficient and less expensive,

81
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:49,120
that's when that came on the scene.

82
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:52,940
And, of course, as we moved to the suburbs and yards became bigger,

83
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:54,880
the equipment became bigger.

84
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:00,420
Rotary lawnmowers cut the grass using a blade spinning like a fan

85
00:07:00,421 --> 00:07:02,080
completely underneath the mower.

86
00:07:02,081 --> 00:07:05,320
a safer and more efficient method than its predecessors.

87
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:11,400
Yet their heavy cast iron engines made them difficult to push across the lawn.

88
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:16,520
Briggs & Stratton's contribution to the world, I guess you could say,

89
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:22,600
was the invention of the aluminum engine, which Briggs & Stratton introduced in 1954.

90
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:27,160
Yet the gasoline mower wasn't the only game in town.

91
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:32,600
Electric mowers began to gain in popularity during the late 60s and early 70s.

92
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:38,520
Soon, there was either a gas-powered or electric lawnmower suited for every type of lawn.

93
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:46,280
The benefits of a power mower over an electric is that you're not encumbered by a cord or battery life,

94
00:07:46,700 --> 00:07:50,080
and you can use more attachments, and you can cut bigger lawns.

95
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:53,260
The disadvantage of a power mower, of course, is the noise.

96
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:56,060
Electric mower, you can just turn a switch.

97
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:57,920
You don't have any battery or gas to fill with.

98
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:00,380
And they're fine for very, very small lawns.

99
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:04,960
But for the average suburban lawn today, it's definitely power.

100
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:10,900
The chore of getting rid of the cut grass has also been eliminated.

101
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:14,080
As modern mowers chew cuttings into mulch.

102
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:19,620
It cuts the grass up into very small particles and puts it back into the lawn.

103
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:21,500
And that actually is like fertilizer.

104
00:08:21,501 --> 00:08:23,920
It's nature's way of re-fertilizing the lawn.

105
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:30,500
Some modern lawn tractors have as much plastic as steel in their construction

106
00:08:30,501 --> 00:08:34,140
and are styled for comfort and ease of use, much like an automobile.

107
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:39,020
The styling is very automotive type styling.

108
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:41,100
Sleek, comfortable seats.

109
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:43,500
There are springs that give you a nice cushion ride.

110
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:49,820
And these babies can turn on the proverbial dime, literally.

111
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:54,780
It's amazing. You go out and cut the grass on a Sunday afternoon.

112
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:57,240
It's like you're taking a drive on your lawn on a Sunday afternoon.

113
00:08:57,580 --> 00:08:58,880
They're really neat.

114
00:08:58,881 --> 00:09:02,440
I just got one on my lawn, and it's drawn a lot of attention to the neighborhood.

115
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:03,460
Guys like it.

116
00:09:08,620 --> 00:09:12,340
Some owners have customized their tractors into mini racing machines.

117
00:09:13,140 --> 00:09:17,160
And like a hot car, tractor lovers like to show off their rigs.

118
00:09:17,161 --> 00:09:23,280
At the other end of the lawn mowing spectrum are the robot lawn mowers.

119
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:29,900
Around since the 1930s, remote control or robot lawn mowers move about the lawn by themselves,

120
00:09:30,660 --> 00:09:33,800
thus completely eliminating all physical exertion.

121
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:41,360
As we speak, we're testing some small little robot mowers that you bury a wire around the perimeter of the yard

122
00:09:41,361 --> 00:09:45,980
and the thing randomly runs back and forth like a kid's toy, cutting the grass.

123
00:09:45,981 --> 00:09:51,120
and there are some solar powered ones which are great as long as the sun shines the thing is

124
00:09:51,121 --> 00:09:56,480
cutting all the time i suspect one of the big issues was in the security it's worth a few bucks

125
00:09:56,481 --> 00:10:02,580
and somebody's going to steal it if you're not watching and it takes a long time to cut all the

126
00:10:02,581 --> 00:10:10,040
grass but you're essentially cutting grass all day long whether by robot mower or rideable lawn

127
00:10:10,041 --> 00:10:16,680
tractor, once the grass is cut, it's time to relax. And relaxation is defined by one

128
00:10:16,681 --> 00:10:21,775
word in the backyards of summertime America. Barbecue.

129
00:10:21,776 --> 00:10:25,360
Like the maintenance of the homestead,

130
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:30,327
the responsibility of outdoor cooking usually falls on the man of the family.

131
00:10:30,328 --> 00:10:31,028
Hence, the

132
00:10:31,029 --> 00:10:35,780
barbecue grill is found to place in most of America's garages and is a source of pride

133
00:10:35,781 --> 00:10:38,020
for the carnivores of the 21st century.

134
00:10:39,860 --> 00:10:42,020
About 25,000 years ago,

135
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:43,580
a man discovered fire,

136
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:45,080
and later that same day,

137
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:46,480
probably around suppertime,

138
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:48,320
he discovered barbecue.

139
00:10:49,140 --> 00:10:52,340
Barbecue then was pretty much the same as it is today.

140
00:10:52,460 --> 00:10:54,000
It's sort of a mystical communion

141
00:10:54,001 --> 00:10:57,760
of fire, smoke, and meat.

142
00:10:58,300 --> 00:11:00,500
I mean, it was almost perfect from the beginning.

143
00:11:01,060 --> 00:11:02,400
That's one of the few things

144
00:11:02,401 --> 00:11:04,260
we haven't screwed up through evolution.

145
00:11:05,780 --> 00:11:16,431
When the Spanish explorers first came to the West Indies, they found that the natives of the West Indies did a meal called barbacoa, which was really meats that were

146
00:11:16,432 --> 00:11:17,980
grilled over open coals.

147
00:11:18,480 --> 00:11:28,799
So the word really comes from the West Indies, and the culture of grilling over coals was something that spread throughout the North American continent, and of course

148
00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:30,540
is with us today quite well.

149
00:11:30,541 --> 00:11:36,900
One of the drawbacks to the original barbecue grills was the open fire needed to cook the food.

150
00:11:39,180 --> 00:11:45,100
But interestingly enough, it was the king of the automobile, an avid camper and outdoor cooking enthusiast,

151
00:11:45,101 --> 00:11:49,720
that helped make the barbecue a more mobile and less dangerous cooking option.

152
00:11:51,300 --> 00:11:54,320
Actually, the Chocco briquette came from Henry Ford.

153
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:58,880
Henry Ford used wood frames in some of the original Model Ts,

154
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:02,600
and they ended up with a tremendous amount of scraps of wood in his factory.

155
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:06,120
So Henry being the entrepreneur and inventor that he was,

156
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:09,300
and also one that watched the dollars and cents of manufacturing,

157
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:12,180
said, what are we going to do with all of this scrap wood?

158
00:12:12,181 --> 00:12:13,540
And he came up with the idea.

159
00:12:14,080 --> 00:12:14,780
Now, wait a minute.

160
00:12:14,781 --> 00:12:19,620
If we can make a fuel that people could use for picnicking, it would do two things.

161
00:12:20,020 --> 00:12:22,980
We could take the scrap wood, turn it into charcoal,

162
00:12:22,981 --> 00:12:27,140
and then we could sell it to folks who would decide,

163
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:30,040
I want to go picnicking in the countryside, and how would they get there?

164
00:12:30,120 --> 00:12:35,120
They'd get there in their Ford, so it would increase automobile usage as well.

165
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:41,300
Another key contributor to the evolution of the barbecue was George Stephen,

166
00:12:42,180 --> 00:12:43,840
a metal worker from Chicago.

167
00:12:44,580 --> 00:12:46,840
Like most purveyors of garage gadgets,

168
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:49,900
Stephen was looking for a better way to accomplish a task

169
00:12:49,901 --> 00:12:54,840
when the idea for a new kind of barbecue grill launched an American culinary legend.

170
00:12:56,600 --> 00:13:00,080
George, like many outdoor chefs in the 1950s,

171
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:03,180
was really dissatisfied with the various grills that were out there.

172
00:13:03,280 --> 00:13:06,240
They looked kind of like a big hubcap, very shallow.

173
00:13:06,420 --> 00:13:10,360
They had a little grate over the top, three legs, and there was no control.

174
00:13:10,361 --> 00:13:14,960
When the wind blew, the ashes would blow all over the food, flare-ups would happen.

175
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:19,320
And so George had the idea, would a grill work better if I put a lid on it?

176
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:25,260
The kettle-like design of the Weber grill cooked food more evenly,

177
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:28,740
and the adjustable airflow allowed more heat control for the cook.

178
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:34,760
And while barbecue purists continue to use charcoal briquettes or even hardwood chips,

179
00:13:35,580 --> 00:13:40,060
most modern grills are powered by propane gas and offer a variety of features.

180
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:44,700
We've come a long way from the Weber kettle of the 50s and 60s.

181
00:13:45,100 --> 00:13:48,620
Now you can cook an entire meal out on your back deck.

182
00:13:48,621 --> 00:13:55,200
rotisserie, you got an extra deck up here and a side burner where you can cook your side dishes.

183
00:13:56,500 --> 00:14:00,420
And the great thing is the cleanup is all outside.

184
00:14:02,560 --> 00:14:06,680
Like the lawnmower, the barbecue can be a source of personal pride for its owner.

185
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:11,300
And for some, as with the mower, bigger is better.

186
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:16,100
Well, the Ultimate Grill has a retail selling price of about $6,000.

187
00:14:16,101 --> 00:14:21,540
It weighs approximately 500 pounds of solid stainless steel.

188
00:14:22,060 --> 00:14:24,640
It's guaranteed for life every part of it.

189
00:14:25,980 --> 00:14:31,380
But whether it's cooked on a fully loaded Weber steel grill or a $10 portable,

190
00:14:32,120 --> 00:14:34,600
whether it's seared with charcoal, gas, or electric,

191
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:39,960
count on that barbecue to get you out of the house and into a fine summer afternoon.

192
00:14:39,961 --> 00:14:48,040
Fall is in the air, and that means lots of leaves on the ground when Garage Gadgets returns.

193
00:14:49,740 --> 00:14:52,660
75% of Americans own a barbecue grill.

194
00:14:53,240 --> 00:14:57,740
Together they spend over $400 million a year on charcoal briquettes.

195
00:14:58,800 --> 00:15:00,020
Modern Marvels will continue.

196
00:15:01,780 --> 00:15:05,220
We now return to Garage Gadgets on Modern Marvels.

197
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:17,440
As the days get shorter and the nights get crisper,

198
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,820
the barbecue and lawnmower migrate back to the garage.

199
00:15:21,940 --> 00:15:27,180
It's time for a new set of tools to help the homeowner confront the early signs of old man winter.

200
00:15:27,181 --> 00:15:40,440
beautiful fall foliage creates an unsightly and inconvenient mess in the minds of some homeowners and their families

201
00:15:40,441 --> 00:15:42,920
what to do with all the leaves

202
00:15:42,921 --> 00:15:49,360
for some it's just another chore but for those with the latest garage gadgets

203
00:15:49,361 --> 00:15:54,860
the thrill of blowing sucking and mulching leaves into oblivion is pure satisfaction

204
00:15:54,861 --> 00:16:00,760
For centuries, the garden rake was the preferred tool for gathering fallen leaves.

205
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:06,500
The rake is still a garage staple, but like most tools, has become more efficient.

206
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:12,300
The rakes have changed a lot over the years, and one of the most recent evolutions is a folding rake.

207
00:16:12,380 --> 00:16:15,980
It's called an all-in-one rake, and it's a rake that you don't have to bend over.

208
00:16:16,080 --> 00:16:18,900
You just rake up the leaves, and you pull on a lever on it.

209
00:16:18,901 --> 00:16:21,800
It folds in half and grabs the debris or the leaves,

210
00:16:21,801 --> 00:16:25,660
It allows you to pick them up and drop them into the trash can next to you without bending over.

211
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:34,560
Although the rake would continue to hang on the wall of most garages,

212
00:16:35,180 --> 00:16:38,020
sooner or later consumers would demand bigger and better.

213
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:44,120
Leaf removal, I'm sure, has been going on since man walked upright.

214
00:16:44,780 --> 00:16:51,460
And I believe the legend is that the Japanese use the equivalent of a fireplace bellows to blow the leaves.

215
00:16:51,800 --> 00:16:55,440
off, I'm sure they weren't driveways, but they're paths or whatever.

216
00:16:56,640 --> 00:17:01,800
I believe that necessity is the mother of all inventions.

217
00:17:02,380 --> 00:17:06,040
A good example would be the power blowers.

218
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:12,860
The power blowers really truly did not exist before 1972.

219
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:16,620
They did and they did not.

220
00:17:16,621 --> 00:17:22,780
Power blowers did exist, but the company that manufactured them just didn't know it.

221
00:17:24,120 --> 00:17:29,520
Echo Incorporated had successfully manufactured this gas-powered garden insecticide sprayer

222
00:17:29,521 --> 00:17:32,500
when a large number of the fluid tanks were returned.

223
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:36,440
I couldn't find anything wrong with them.

224
00:17:36,441 --> 00:17:37,846
I called the distributor.

225
00:17:37,847 --> 00:17:41,840
He goes, well, there's a couple guys that are buying the Echo sprayers,

226
00:17:41,841 --> 00:17:47,280
And they're removing the tanks and they're putting a piece of metal on the top where the hole is now.

227
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:55,020
Gardeners owe a debt of gratitude to the California landscapers who modified the Echo Power sprayers to function as leaf blowers.

228
00:17:55,600 --> 00:18:01,260
Like modern blowers, these machines removed leaves and grass cuttings much faster than a rake or broom.

229
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:11,380
My boss went to California and actually watched the people using, you may say, their world's first power blowers in the street.

230
00:18:11,381 --> 00:18:17,340
And they've asked us if we could manufacture something directly from the factory, which we did.

231
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:21,780
And the world's first power blower was born, which was called a PB9.

232
00:18:23,800 --> 00:18:27,700
Blowers became a favorite tool of professional landscapers and homeowners.

233
00:18:28,560 --> 00:18:33,360
But noisy blowers ruined neighbor relations as fast as the wind blew out of them.

234
00:18:34,320 --> 00:18:36,500
Many communities have outlawed leaf blowers.

235
00:18:36,501 --> 00:18:46,780
If you make the blower extremely efficient, you've also made the blower noisy because the fan starts ripping the air just like you have on the siren.

236
00:18:47,580 --> 00:18:49,240
But that can be overcome.

237
00:18:49,980 --> 00:18:55,500
The new designs and shapes of the fans, the new designs and the shapes of the fan housings,

238
00:18:55,501 --> 00:19:03,100
housings, the way that you cover the unit to insulate the unit from noise, is some of

239
00:19:03,101 --> 00:19:09,460
the technologies that has been incorporating in their new state-of-the-art blowers that

240
00:19:09,461 --> 00:19:10,720
are sold in the marketplace.

241
00:19:12,100 --> 00:19:15,300
Once the leaves have been gathered, they must be disposed of.

242
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:21,720
In the old days, people burned piles of autumn leaves in a ritual less viable in a more environmentally

243
00:19:21,721 --> 00:19:22,700
conscious America.

244
00:19:22,701 --> 00:19:28,300
that's really the hard part of the leaf removal depending over dragging it out

245
00:19:28,301 --> 00:19:34,380
we have a disposal issue and if you have a fair-sized yard with a lot of trees

246
00:19:34,381 --> 00:19:40,740
you probably want to get some kind of a shredding device and from chippers and shredders and

247
00:19:40,741 --> 00:19:46,600
vacuums and shredders and vacuums and shredders and baggers we finally got to where all former

248
00:19:46,601 --> 00:19:48,660
in one. We have a new device called

249
00:19:48,661 --> 00:19:50,860
a vacuum chipper

250
00:19:50,861 --> 00:19:51,940
shredder bagger.

251
00:20:01,020 --> 00:20:02,760
Autumn also means preparing

252
00:20:02,761 --> 00:20:04,740
for a long, cold winter ahead.

253
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:06,640
That has often meant

254
00:20:06,641 --> 00:20:07,700
chopping firewood.

255
00:20:10,260 --> 00:20:12,860
A crisp air of fall often echoes

256
00:20:12,861 --> 00:20:15,120
with the familiar whine of the chainsaw.

257
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:23,360
Before the invention of chainsaws, the work of taking down a tree was done with axes,

258
00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:27,000
or two-handed cross-cut saws.

259
00:20:28,340 --> 00:20:32,020
The back-breaking, labor-intensive work demanded a more efficient solution.

260
00:20:35,440 --> 00:20:40,920
In the 1800s, we had guys that were using machinery to power their saw blades,

261
00:20:40,921 --> 00:20:47,600
But it wasn't until 1910 that finally a guy had the idea to put little saw teeth on a chain.

262
00:20:48,300 --> 00:20:53,920
And then it was a guy named Andres Still, a very famous name now, who was actually a loafer by avocation.

263
00:20:54,860 --> 00:20:59,040
Hung out in the bar with a lot of loggers, saw the back-breaking work they did.

264
00:20:59,660 --> 00:21:08,000
He put that idea together with the power saw and came up with the chainsaw, which was a powered chain that would cut.

265
00:21:08,001 --> 00:21:12,800
and the problem was it weighed 147 pounds.

266
00:21:13,340 --> 00:21:16,280
The cutting chain of the gas-powered saw was faster

267
00:21:16,281 --> 00:21:20,360
but required a two-man crew and a great deal of maintenance to operate.

268
00:21:24,020 --> 00:21:27,600
Just after World War II, the company developed a lighter engine

269
00:21:27,601 --> 00:21:31,120
and the first one-man consumer chainsaws hit the market.

270
00:21:34,340 --> 00:21:37,780
The next cutting innovation came from an unlikely source.

271
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:39,400
A timber beetle.

272
00:21:40,120 --> 00:21:45,700
In 1947, logger inventor Joseph Buford Cox came up with a better cutting chain.

273
00:21:46,840 --> 00:21:55,580
What he did is he spent hours observing wood beetle, how it chews up a piece of wood.

274
00:21:56,300 --> 00:22:02,060
And he designed what was called the first scratcher type of a chain.

275
00:22:02,061 --> 00:22:09,220
Cox's beetle-inspired cutting jaw remains a basic feature for chainsaw manufacturers today.

276
00:22:11,640 --> 00:22:16,640
And as the saw became lighter, big names like Sears marketed to the homeowner.

277
00:22:17,460 --> 00:22:21,640
By the mid-60s, an electric chainsaw weighed as little as 11 pounds.

278
00:22:23,900 --> 00:22:30,600
Sharp rises in oil prices in the 1970s and early 80s caused a mini-boom in chainsaw sales.

279
00:22:30,601 --> 00:22:36,200
as homeowners looked to wood burning as an alternative fuel to oil for heating their homes.

280
00:22:41,520 --> 00:22:45,740
Despite their convenience, early chainsaws were dangerous tools.

281
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:51,280
If a spinning saw is snagged in a log, it could violently lurch back and cut the user.

282
00:22:51,920 --> 00:22:55,920
You're always susceptible to a kickback when you're using a chainsaw

283
00:22:55,921 --> 00:22:58,860
and people have suffered serious injuries.

284
00:22:58,861 --> 00:23:10,160
The auto brake was a real critical device as far as safety goes, and that's, I think, the biggest single improvement to the chainsaw is the auto brake.

285
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:15,100
The auto brake works off the inertia of the cutting chain.

286
00:23:15,740 --> 00:23:23,180
If the blade snags in the wood, the brake mechanism trips and locks the chain from cutting, thus preventing any unwanted cuts.

287
00:23:28,860 --> 00:23:36,300
Today, the original chainsaw design has evolved into various hybrids that were developed to adhere to specific consumer demands.

288
00:23:37,960 --> 00:23:47,300
Probably the biggest change occur where the cutting attachment is not in the operator's hands.

289
00:23:49,800 --> 00:23:58,480
Power pruners and tree trimmers have evolved from the original chainsaw design to allow anyone, anywhere, to cut just about anything.

290
00:23:58,860 --> 00:24:07,240
The chainsaw has certainly evolved from a behemoth machine that took two burly lumberjacks to operate.

291
00:24:08,040 --> 00:24:13,620
Like most garage tools, it has become smaller and lighter, yet packing more power than ever before.

292
00:24:19,740 --> 00:24:23,280
Next, garage gadgets take on old man winter.

293
00:24:23,281 --> 00:24:32,400
Chainsaws cause over 40,000 injuries or deaths in the United States each year.

294
00:24:34,180 --> 00:24:37,480
Garage Gadgets will return on Modern Marvels.

295
00:24:39,980 --> 00:24:43,960
We now return to Garage Gadgets on Modern Marvels.

296
00:24:45,840 --> 00:24:46,680
Winter.

297
00:24:47,720 --> 00:24:50,440
All that beautiful white stuff on the ground.

298
00:24:51,280 --> 00:24:52,940
But what if you have to move it?

299
00:24:53,280 --> 00:25:05,680
In the early days, fallen snow was either shoveled by hand or pushed to the side by horse-drawn carriage.

300
00:25:07,360 --> 00:25:10,120
Snow shoveling is a terrible task.

301
00:25:11,120 --> 00:25:13,960
A few inches of light snow is not such a problem.

302
00:25:13,961 --> 00:25:20,120
But when you get several inches, four, six or more of heavy wet snow, it's a real task.

303
00:25:20,121 --> 00:25:26,120
And so the idea of having a piece of powered equipment to do that job for you is very appealing.

304
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:32,260
You can trace the history of the snowblower in the garage to Arthur Sicard.

305
00:25:35,900 --> 00:25:40,380
In 1925, the Montreal inventor created the first snowblower,

306
00:25:40,740 --> 00:25:47,860
which consisted of a four-wheel drive truck chassis and a snow scooper with a chute that blew snow 90 feet away.

307
00:25:50,120 --> 00:25:55,420
Over time, a spinning corkscrew blade was developed that scraped up the snow as it moved forward.

308
00:25:56,760 --> 00:25:59,400
This blade would then funnel the snow into a chute.

309
00:26:02,380 --> 00:26:06,760
Compressed air from the engine would then force the snow through the chute and away from the machine.

310
00:26:06,761 --> 00:26:20,761
By the 1950s, compact, easy-to-use blowers became available to the consumer for under $100.

311
00:26:26,780 --> 00:26:31,120
When the price of the product got to the point where it was very affordable,

312
00:26:31,121 --> 00:26:36,980
is a very attractive thing to those of us who live in the northern part of the United States.

313
00:26:38,500 --> 00:26:44,240
Like most garage tools, the design of snowblowers began to feature the needs and wants of the common homeowner.

314
00:26:45,420 --> 00:26:50,200
We have more and better features, for example, heated grips so that your hands don't get cold.

315
00:26:50,201 --> 00:26:52,980
They've got electric chute rotation.

316
00:26:53,240 --> 00:26:59,680
We've got pitch control, so you don't have to loosen up nuts and bolts to put the pitch of the chute to aim the snow.

317
00:26:59,681 --> 00:27:02,480
You can do that right from the driver's dash.

318
00:27:05,160 --> 00:27:10,020
Like the lawnmower in summer, bigger and faster snowblowers keep hitting the market.

319
00:27:10,900 --> 00:27:17,080
Some of these beasts can clear 18 inches of new snow from a 30-foot-long two-car driveway in 30 minutes.

320
00:27:18,080 --> 00:27:24,680
And for those who already own a lawn tractor, new systems of plow and snowblower attachments are available for snow removal.

321
00:27:29,680 --> 00:27:35,620
With the snow cleared, winter offers an opportunity to just stay inside.

322
00:27:37,060 --> 00:27:40,320
It's the time to employ the garage as a household workshop

323
00:27:40,321 --> 00:27:44,040
for woodworking, home improvement, and other indoor projects.

324
00:27:45,220 --> 00:27:49,680
If we looked at an older garage, say a 1930s or 1940s garage,

325
00:27:49,800 --> 00:27:52,540
the type of workbench and storage system that you might find there

326
00:27:52,541 --> 00:27:55,080
is typically something that somebody built for themselves,

327
00:27:55,620 --> 00:27:58,260
so they may have custom-fitted into the corner of the garage.

328
00:27:59,680 --> 00:28:09,680
But as we move on to more contemporary product,

329
00:28:09,800 --> 00:28:12,960
what we find is that storage systems are available, ready-made.

330
00:28:14,540 --> 00:28:16,440
Organization is the name of the game,

331
00:28:16,960 --> 00:28:20,980
and manufacturers have responded by creating workbenches and toolboxes

332
00:28:20,981 --> 00:28:23,840
that are designed to fit in the confines of the modern garage.

333
00:28:26,460 --> 00:28:27,900
This is a very large unit.

334
00:28:28,020 --> 00:28:29,120
It costs almost $3,500,

335
00:28:29,680 --> 00:28:31,840
It's almost large enough that you could live inside of it.

336
00:28:32,120 --> 00:28:35,720
It has a locker on the side where you have space for large items.

337
00:28:36,580 --> 00:28:41,680
It has a series of drawers, including articulating fronts that lock in place,

338
00:28:41,880 --> 00:28:45,080
so that when you close the drawer in place,

339
00:28:45,440 --> 00:28:49,440
if you move this box up against something and jar it, the drawers won't come open on their own.

340
00:28:50,060 --> 00:28:53,320
We move from that to a much smaller unit that's very flexible and portable.

341
00:28:53,940 --> 00:28:58,220
This is a project center that moves around on wheels just like this larger system does.

342
00:28:58,221 --> 00:29:01,060
But in this case, we also have storage drawers underneath.

343
00:29:01,760 --> 00:29:06,740
We have these plastic bumpers on the side so that if you run this into the car as you're working in the garage,

344
00:29:07,360 --> 00:29:09,260
you won't mar the finish of the automobile.

345
00:29:09,880 --> 00:29:17,140
This has storage on top, and on top, a sliding galvanized top that's impervious to liquids and solvents

346
00:29:17,141 --> 00:29:19,520
and storage for small parts inside.

347
00:29:21,460 --> 00:29:25,220
The workbench, considered the altar of the American garage,

348
00:29:25,221 --> 00:29:27,940
is the platform from which many projects begin.

349
00:29:28,220 --> 00:29:34,100
But many don't have room for a full-size bench, so new work platforms developed.

350
00:29:35,420 --> 00:29:39,760
The workmate was invented by Englishman Ron Hickman in 1961.

351
00:29:40,880 --> 00:29:47,140
On his day off as an automotive designer, he accidentally sawed through a dining room chair he was using to support a project.

352
00:29:48,980 --> 00:29:51,160
Inspired, he welded together a frame.

353
00:29:51,500 --> 00:29:57,380
He then fastened a bench top of two separate boards, leaving a gap for his saw to pass through.

354
00:29:58,220 --> 00:30:03,000
Over the years, Hickman continued to improve his invention and even sold it through mail order.

355
00:30:04,780 --> 00:30:07,680
Eventually, Black & Decker took over sales of the devices,

356
00:30:08,220 --> 00:30:11,480
and the Workmate workbench became a staple garage gadget.

357
00:30:12,520 --> 00:30:20,040
It's portable, it's lightweight, it's sturdy, and it's very versatile as far as being able to clamp.

358
00:30:20,520 --> 00:30:25,880
You can use it as a sawhorse, you can use it as a vice, and so it has many, many applications.

359
00:30:28,220 --> 00:30:35,100
Every garage owner knows how important it is to save space.

360
00:30:35,840 --> 00:30:40,540
After all, you can fill up that saved space with even more tools and gadgets.

361
00:30:46,640 --> 00:30:51,180
Next, spring is the time for whacking, trimming, and spraying.

362
00:30:51,181 --> 00:31:01,580
During the 1920s, snow shovelers in Montreal hid crowbars in the city's snowy streets

363
00:31:01,581 --> 00:31:04,300
to stop the new mechanical snow throwers.

364
00:31:05,820 --> 00:31:08,420
Garage Gadgets will return on Modern Marvels.

365
00:31:10,560 --> 00:31:14,260
We now return to Garage Gadgets on Modern Marvels.

366
00:31:16,400 --> 00:31:18,580
The sleeping ground awakes.

367
00:31:19,540 --> 00:31:24,640
Spring means vegetation that bolts to life, and that means it's time to whack the weeds.

368
00:31:25,660 --> 00:31:35,540
Prior to string trimmers, there really wasn't much to trim the grass around flower beds or edged around the sidewalks.

369
00:31:35,541 --> 00:31:40,700
Of course, there was a time-consuming work because you had to go back and forth, back and forth,

370
00:31:41,160 --> 00:31:44,540
until you've got maybe a foot of nice, clean cut.

371
00:31:44,541 --> 00:31:49,600
The time was ripe for a more effective solution to unwanted growth.

372
00:31:50,420 --> 00:31:53,940
Intrigued by the spinning motion of the bristles in an automatic car wash,

373
00:31:55,000 --> 00:32:00,040
George Ballas, a Houston dance teacher, revolutionized the art of weed whacking forever.

374
00:32:02,120 --> 00:32:08,820
Mr. Ballas' idea was that nylon line can actually cut grass.

375
00:32:08,821 --> 00:32:17,873
What he did is he took a popcorn can, he punched the holes through that popcorn can and tie lined to it.

376
00:32:17,874 --> 00:32:25,360
Utilizing the Echoes brush cutter that he adapted, that's how he received his patents.

377
00:32:25,361 --> 00:32:39,360
The world's first string trimmer, then, was manufactured in the United States, and it was powered by our engine, which was a Weedeater 607, a Weedeater 608.

378
00:32:39,361 --> 00:32:53,361
Wrapped around a spinning head, a small length of nylon line could cut any stubborn weed or patch of grass instantly, and was a much improved tool over the steel blade

379
00:32:55,680 --> 00:32:56,560
trimmers.

380
00:32:56,561 --> 00:33:02,260
Weed eaters, or string trimmers as they came to be called, swept the nation.

381
00:33:02,261 --> 00:33:09,860
The first string trimmers that really hit the market that were very popular in the United States

382
00:33:09,861 --> 00:33:14,240
were string trimmers that were electric-powered string trimmers.

383
00:33:14,241 --> 00:33:23,360
You had to connect sometimes 50, 75 to 100 feet of cord and pull it behind you to get the trimming job done.

384
00:33:23,880 --> 00:33:28,120
Of course, for a commercial application, that would never work.

385
00:33:28,121 --> 00:33:35,780
so when the small gasoline engine power trimmers came out on the market

386
00:33:35,781 --> 00:33:38,120
they took the market by the storm

387
00:33:38,121 --> 00:33:41,180
you no longer had to worry about the cord

388
00:33:41,181 --> 00:33:48,500
manufacturers have always strived to make their tools multifunctional

389
00:33:48,501 --> 00:33:50,940
to wreak as much garden havoc as possible

390
00:33:50,941 --> 00:34:01,320
where the industry evolved today is making tools that are multifunctional

391
00:34:01,321 --> 00:34:08,180
well we make a bunch of attachments we make a brush cutter attachment we make a trimmer

392
00:34:08,181 --> 00:34:15,320
attachment we make a hedge clipper attachment we make extended chainsaw attachment dethatching

393
00:34:15,321 --> 00:34:16,458
attachments.

394
00:34:16,459 --> 00:34:23,760
So now, the beauty of the system is that the customer has only one engine and

395
00:34:23,761 --> 00:34:26,820
one power source to worry about.

396
00:34:26,821 --> 00:34:32,080
Someday soon, all of these multi-purpose tools may make

397
00:34:32,081 --> 00:34:39,331
your local hardware store a lot smaller and the room in your garage a lot bigger.

398
00:34:39,332 --> 00:34:40,032
Not

399
00:34:40,033 --> 00:34:44,040
all the tools in the garage are for use in the yard. Yet another set of gadgets help

400
00:34:44,041 --> 00:34:47,120
with a classic springtime chore, painting the house.

401
00:34:55,520 --> 00:34:58,720
In the beginning, we applied paint with our fingers,

402
00:34:59,120 --> 00:35:02,740
those early berries that they spread onto the cave walls.

403
00:35:03,740 --> 00:35:06,020
Soon they discovered that they could use bristles.

404
00:35:06,021 --> 00:35:09,660
They might come from an animal or they might come from a plant source.

405
00:35:09,661 --> 00:35:13,620
and then they were tying bristles onto a wooden stick.

406
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:19,220
For thousands of years, household painting was done by hand.

407
00:35:19,900 --> 00:35:23,900
First by brushing and then later by rolling paint onto a surface.

408
00:35:25,380 --> 00:35:29,180
And then sometime in the 60s, a guy named Wagner came up with a great invention.

409
00:35:29,181 --> 00:35:34,720
You could actually attach to a regular can of paint a device, the power painter,

410
00:35:35,180 --> 00:35:38,080
that would turn this into a spray painter.

411
00:35:38,081 --> 00:35:41,720
so you could do your wall in a whole lot less time.

412
00:35:43,380 --> 00:35:47,980
With power sprayers, technology once only available to paint professionals

413
00:35:47,981 --> 00:35:49,740
was introduced to the rest of us.

414
00:35:53,540 --> 00:35:56,800
Household sprayers mixed liquid paint with compressed air

415
00:35:56,801 --> 00:36:00,760
and funneled both into a long tube ending in a narrow nozzle.

416
00:36:01,720 --> 00:36:03,740
Painting the house suddenly became more fun.

417
00:36:03,741 --> 00:36:13,660
The same air compressor that applies your paint is also the garage tool that became the primary power source for a new generation of tools.

418
00:36:15,960 --> 00:36:23,580
Early on in the Industrial Revolution, manufacturers discovered that they needed compressed air for smelting and metal manufacturing.

419
00:36:25,280 --> 00:36:30,360
As a result, air compressors became a staple on most every factory floor.

420
00:36:31,060 --> 00:36:35,680
In these power-driven machines, air is compressed by a moving piston in a cylinder.

421
00:36:36,640 --> 00:36:41,580
The pressure is increased by reducing the volume of space, and the compressed air is forced out.

422
00:36:43,480 --> 00:36:49,620
Today, compact oil-less air compressors supply a tremendous amount of power in a relatively tiny package.

423
00:36:52,200 --> 00:37:00,120
The air compressor being the power source, as opposed to AC electric or gasoline-powered tool,

424
00:37:00,360 --> 00:37:03,460
or battery-powered tool, you have a pneumatic-powered tool.

425
00:37:03,600 --> 00:37:09,240
It has really come on the marketplace from the standpoint not of the air compressor itself,

426
00:37:09,680 --> 00:37:18,400
but from all of the accessories or the tools, the pneumatic tools that have been developed for the air compressor.

427
00:37:20,240 --> 00:37:28,700
With modern portable air compressors, garage dwellers can screw, drill, and cut just like the pros do, at a much lower cost.

428
00:37:30,360 --> 00:37:38,860
Another item that has evolved from industrial use is the gas-powered pressure washer.

429
00:37:39,500 --> 00:37:43,820
The newest models are easy to use and operate like a do-it-yourself car wash.

430
00:37:44,680 --> 00:37:50,400
Settings for suds or rinse make cleaning up the rest of your gadgets a much easier task.

431
00:38:00,360 --> 00:38:04,480
Coming up, see what the future brings to Garage Gadgets.

432
00:38:07,880 --> 00:38:12,640
Weedeater manufactures 720 million feet of trimmer line per year,

433
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:15,920
enough to wrap around the earth five and a half times.

434
00:38:17,320 --> 00:38:19,980
Garage Gadgets will return on Modern Marvels.

435
00:38:23,960 --> 00:38:29,040
When you're done using your tools, you need a heavy-duty garage door to keep them safe.

436
00:38:29,780 --> 00:38:32,700
What better way to move that door than with another gadget?

437
00:38:35,840 --> 00:38:41,680
One of the first garage gadgets was the automatic garage door opener, patented in 1922.

438
00:38:44,840 --> 00:38:49,160
First ones were some kind of a plate in the driveway that sensed your car was there

439
00:38:49,161 --> 00:38:51,560
and triggered a big spring that pulled the door open.

440
00:38:52,260 --> 00:38:53,940
I find that kind of intriguing.

441
00:38:54,520 --> 00:38:59,760
Obviously, there are security issues, and you could probably do some serious damage to somebody

442
00:38:59,761 --> 00:39:02,260
if they were standing on the other side when this door opened.

443
00:39:05,820 --> 00:39:10,960
Addressing the security issue, contemporary magazines in the 30s published plans

444
00:39:10,961 --> 00:39:15,880
for a key-operated garage door opener using a Ford starter motor and a counterweight.

445
00:39:15,881 --> 00:39:26,000
As a variety of automatic opening systems using motorized chains, screw, and cable mechanisms developed,

446
00:39:26,460 --> 00:39:29,700
the technology of the day was reflected in the garage door.

447
00:39:30,820 --> 00:39:35,220
The original design of garage door openers as we currently know them

448
00:39:35,221 --> 00:39:38,440
were driven by the equivalent of a bicycle chain.

449
00:39:39,520 --> 00:39:42,740
There's a sprocket on the head or the motor of the unit,

450
00:39:42,741 --> 00:39:46,160
and it just drags that chain around like a bicycle sprocket does.

451
00:39:46,680 --> 00:39:49,940
And the other end is affixed to the garage door and pulls it up.

452
00:39:52,260 --> 00:39:55,100
Over the years, the convenience of the automatic opener

453
00:39:55,101 --> 00:39:57,360
made it a widely popular garage gadget.

454
00:39:57,361 --> 00:40:11,361
Remote control systems in the 1960s utilized a primitive code method

455
00:40:13,120 --> 00:40:16,640
that would electronically open your garage door with the push of a finger.

456
00:40:17,360 --> 00:40:23,180
Unfortunately, it also allowed one in five remote controls on the market to open your garage door.

457
00:40:23,181 --> 00:40:31,580
you had a binary where you had four or five codes that opened all the garage doors and you and your

458
00:40:31,581 --> 00:40:37,700
neighbor may have a struggle with that then we went to electronics where you had dip switches

459
00:40:37,701 --> 00:40:43,620
for want of a better term and you were allowed to pick one of uh seems to be a thousand codes or so

460
00:40:43,621 --> 00:40:52,820
the new rolling code every time you activate the operator the code steps one step in some direction

461
00:40:52,821 --> 00:40:58,760
so that if you have a dishonest type individual out in the street trying to grab your code,

462
00:40:59,200 --> 00:41:03,020
it does them no good because the next time you activate it, it's using another code.

463
00:41:03,440 --> 00:41:07,320
It's called the rolling code technology, and it's an extremely secure system.

464
00:41:11,340 --> 00:41:17,960
Today, the doormaster from Wayne Dalton operates with a special load-sensing geared winder unit

465
00:41:17,961 --> 00:41:19,900
located at the top of the door,

466
00:41:19,901 --> 00:41:27,620
thus eliminating the bulky ceiling-mounted motor, overhead rails, chains, and screw or cable systems.

467
00:41:29,760 --> 00:41:34,740
If people are telling you that they need their garage to do certain things or to meet their needs,

468
00:41:35,100 --> 00:41:38,080
you try to offer them products and features that let them do that.

469
00:41:39,740 --> 00:41:44,400
Garage door openers, today they come with passive infrared remote switches

470
00:41:44,401 --> 00:41:47,380
that can turn the lights in the garage on and off for people automatically

471
00:41:47,381 --> 00:41:50,100
as they walk in and out of the garage so you don't have to hit the switch.

472
00:41:50,620 --> 00:41:52,280
And that's a direct result of people saying,

473
00:41:52,360 --> 00:41:54,980
you know, I wish when I went in my garage this would happen for me

474
00:41:54,981 --> 00:41:58,420
so I didn't have to go walk all the way over to the side and turn the switch on.

475
00:42:00,980 --> 00:42:03,700
The future presents a plethora of garage gadgets

476
00:42:03,701 --> 00:42:07,420
that may tempt the unapologizing consumer on all of us.

477
00:42:08,180 --> 00:42:10,080
One such gadget is the car capsule,

478
00:42:10,540 --> 00:42:15,180
a giant plastic bubble that you drive your car into and inflate with air.

479
00:42:15,720 --> 00:42:19,820
Once inflated, the fan changes the filtered air in the capsule several times an hour,

480
00:42:20,320 --> 00:42:23,360
thus providing a positive airflow that keeps out moisture.

481
00:42:25,420 --> 00:42:30,360
At the same time, the PVC plastic capsule protects the car from dents and scratches.

482
00:42:37,920 --> 00:42:42,960
Another device designed to prevent dents and scratches to the family car is the Park Zone.

483
00:42:42,961 --> 00:42:52,020
This high-tech version of the ball and string uses sensors and a stop-and-go light to indicate when you've driven the car in far enough.

484
00:42:56,120 --> 00:42:59,580
Do you think you need this to reduce your chance of getting a flat tire?

485
00:43:00,800 --> 00:43:03,900
It'll pick up nails and screws from the garage floor.

486
00:43:03,901 --> 00:43:14,120
The magnetic sweeper may also eliminate the tedious task of removing metal debris from grass, asphalt, or whatever your work surface.

487
00:43:15,180 --> 00:43:19,020
A first line of defense for your tires, as well as your family's bare feet.

488
00:43:26,540 --> 00:43:30,600
A problem for most garages is where to put all this stuff.

489
00:43:30,601 --> 00:43:44,400
Sometimes the only space left gadget free is the ceiling.

490
00:43:44,401 --> 00:43:49,540
This hoisting system allows for easy raising and lowering of heavy objects so that they

491
00:43:49,541 --> 00:43:57,840
can be stored above your head and out of the way.

492
00:43:57,841 --> 00:44:04,640
But as we make more space in the garage, one can only wonder what gadgets will fill the garage of the future.

493
00:44:05,640 --> 00:44:14,340
It may be in the future that it'll all be one device, your remote control robot lawnmower paint sprayer, leaf blower,

494
00:44:14,341 --> 00:44:24,260
and you'll sit in your chair out in the yard and sip your tea and poke in some little numbers into your personal digital assistant,

495
00:44:24,261 --> 00:44:29,260
and it will send your little robot all around doing all your work,

496
00:44:29,460 --> 00:44:30,940
and it won't be nearly as much fun.

